WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:05.009
Even with a bottomless budget and a bunch
of A-list stars at Disney's disposal, the

00:00:05.009 --> 00:00:09.800
Pirates of the Caribbean movies may be pretty
great — but they're still not perfect.

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:14.599
Set over the course of several years during
the 18th century, this swashbuckler epic series

00:00:14.599 --> 00:00:19.800
has fallen victim to a number of historical
inaccuracies...though they pale in comparison

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:24.449
to the many rookie mistakes made on the set
and in the editing room.

00:00:24.449 --> 00:00:29.849
From glaring errors in continuity to makeup
mishaps and green screen nightmares, here

00:00:29.849 --> 00:00:34.700
are the biggest mistakes lurking in the Pirates
of the Caribbean movies.

00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:35.900
Jack's escape

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:41.170
In The Curse of the Black Pearl, when Captain
Jack Sparrow is repaid for saving Elizabeth's

00:00:41.170 --> 00:00:46.190
life with arrest, the Captain engineers the
first of what would become many improbable

00:00:46.190 --> 00:00:48.480
escapes over the course of the franchise.

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:53.231
After putting a pistol to the head of Miss
Swann and ordering her to unshackle him, Sparrow

00:00:53.231 --> 00:00:59.000
grabs a rope and releases the cannon at the
other end, flying into a spin and, eventually,

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:00.160
to freedom.

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:02.780
Before departing he tells Commodore Norrington:

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:03.780
"Gentlemen!

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:05.040
My lady.

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:11.550
You will always remember this as the day you
almost caught Captain Jack Sparrow."

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:17.000
In reality, we would remember it as the day
his men time-traveled into the future to upgrade

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:18.110
their weapons.

00:01:18.110 --> 00:01:23.360
The English gunmen fire at will, each popping
off shot after shot, though rifles at the

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:26.850
time would have needed reloading after every
bullet.

00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:32.500
Repeating rifles like these didn't come into
use until 1862, when they were used by Union

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:34.810
soldiers during the American Civil War.

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:37.250
Still, this movie does have pirate zombies.

00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:41.750
So maybe time travel isn't totally out of
the question.

00:01:41.750 --> 00:01:43.000
Elven tattoo

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:48.500
In another Black Pearl blunder, all-round
do-gooder Will Turner engages in an acrobatic

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:54.440
duel that utilizes all the machinery in the
blacksmith shop to stop Sparrow from escaping.

00:01:54.440 --> 00:02:00.860
When he leans over one to taunt Jack, he exposes
a rather unusual tattoo for 1700s Port Royal:

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:05.200
on his forearm in black ink is the word 'Nine'
in J.R.R.

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:09.759
Tolkien's Elvish Tengwar script, a piece he
and a number of his castmates had done to

00:02:09.759 --> 00:02:13.810
commemorate their time working together on
The Lord of the Rings.

00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:18.290
Orlando Bloom took to Instagram to share an
old photo of the gang showing off their matching

00:02:18.290 --> 00:02:20.650
tats the day they got them done.

00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:24.210
That's all well and good for Orlando Bloom,
super-cool-actor guy.

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:28.780
But Will Turner seems like too much of a square
to get a tattoo — even for something as

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:31.260
nerdy as Lord of the Rings.

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:34.390
Maybe they should've covered that thing with
some makeup.

00:02:34.390 --> 00:02:35.730
The lonely cowboy

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:40.210
The sheer number of people needed to bring
a big-budget franchise film to life means

00:02:40.210 --> 00:02:44.250
it can get a little crowded sometimes, even
on the largest of sets.

00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:48.840
Separating the extras from the many crew members
needed to turn a ship into a working film

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:52.150
set clearly proved to be too much of a challenge
here.

00:02:52.150 --> 00:02:56.290
There's a lot of movement on deck when Jack
Sparrow finally returns to the helm of his

00:02:56.290 --> 00:03:01.239
beloved ship, and the final cut of The Curse
of the Black Pearl relies on this to keep

00:03:01.239 --> 00:03:04.020
you blind from the obvious error in the scene.

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:09.500
For just a single frame, a man dressed in
a modern white t-shirt and tan cowboy hat

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:14.840
can be seen over Captain Jack's shoulder,
staring longingly out to sea as the film goes

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:15.840
on around him.

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:16.990
He probably misses his horse.

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:19.560
"If I had a boat.

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:22.620
I’d go out on the ocean.

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:27.960
And if I had a pony,
I’d ride him on my boat."

00:03:27.970 --> 00:03:29.819
Disappearing fish guts

00:03:29.819 --> 00:03:34.120
In Dead Man's Chest, when Will boards the
Dutchman in search of his father, his rescue

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:36.760
mission fails to go as planned.

00:03:36.760 --> 00:03:42.160
Cursed mutineer Bootstrap Bill Turner is bound
to Davy Jones as a member of his immortal

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:47.700
crew, having agreed to serve 100 years aboard
his ship, and the supernatural tyrant has

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:50.690
no intention of breaking that contract.

00:03:50.690 --> 00:03:55.900
Will is set upon by Jones' gruesome crew,
slicing one of his attackers open as he advances

00:03:55.900 --> 00:03:58.800
and spilling his fishy guts all over the deck.

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:03.489
When the young Turner is knocked down seconds
later, however, all those guts have pretty

00:04:03.489 --> 00:04:04.489
much vanished.

00:04:04.489 --> 00:04:10.600
This is either an obvious continuity error
or producers making sure that Orlando Bloom's

00:04:10.600 --> 00:04:16.160
highly marketable face wouldn't be covered
in rotten intestines — even for a brief

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:17.160
moment.

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:18.789
Tortuga concertina

00:04:18.789 --> 00:04:23.240
The Tortuga bar fight is pretty much everything
you could want out of drunken pirates punching

00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:24.240
each other.

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:28.779
It begins with a disheveled James Norrington
applying to serve under the very pirate he

00:04:28.779 --> 00:04:32.949
once chased halfway across the world, and
it ends with the shamed Commodore getting

00:04:32.949 --> 00:04:35.920
a bottle smashed over his head.

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:41.720
The music playing over the all-out brawl that
takes place in between those two events comes

00:04:41.729 --> 00:04:47.309
from a concertina, one of the most 'piratey'
sounding instruments there is.

00:04:47.309 --> 00:04:48.980
Have a listen!

00:04:55.140 --> 00:04:59.819
Even though that tune really fits the scene
well, its inclusion is pretty factually inaccurate.

00:04:59.819 --> 00:05:05.240
The concertina, a cousin of the accordion,
wasn't actually invented until the next century,

00:05:05.240 --> 00:05:06.360
around 1830.

00:05:06.380 --> 00:05:10.660
Still, there's really never a bad time for
a solid concertina jam.

00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:17.180
The big wheel

00:05:17.199 --> 00:05:22.410
The most memorable Dead Man's Chest scene
might be the big wheel run, in which a sword

00:05:22.410 --> 00:05:27.569
fight between Norrington and Turner ends up
becoming a whole new Disney park ride in the

00:05:27.569 --> 00:05:28.569
making.

00:05:28.569 --> 00:05:32.570
Jack Sparrow also finds himself caught up
in the wheel as it rolls downhill towards

00:05:32.570 --> 00:05:37.939
the ocean, though he's thrown out again before
long, knocked out by a metal bar that, from

00:05:37.939 --> 00:05:42.689
all other angles, looks to be a good foot
above the Captain's head.

00:05:42.689 --> 00:05:45.379
That isn't the biggest mistake in the scene,
however.

00:05:45.379 --> 00:05:49.889
When the wheel finally slows to a stop it
falls to the left, with the axle pointing

00:05:49.889 --> 00:05:50.889
down.

00:05:50.889 --> 00:05:55.669
When Norrington and Turner emerge, the wheel
is somehow the other way up, with the axle

00:05:55.669 --> 00:05:57.069
pointing skyward.

00:05:57.069 --> 00:05:59.490
The self-repairing ship

00:05:59.490 --> 00:06:05.169
Davy Jones' ferry to the afterlife, the Flying
Dutchman, is the most formidable ship to have

00:06:05.169 --> 00:06:10.649
appeared in the franchise thus far, rising
from the deep and making matchsticks out of

00:06:10.649 --> 00:06:12.830
Navy and pirate vessels alike.

00:06:12.830 --> 00:06:18.550
As Jones bears down on Jack Sparrow's Black
Pearl in pursuit of his stolen heart, he orders

00:06:18.550 --> 00:06:19.560
his crew:

00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:23.610
"Send his beloved Pearl back to the depths."

00:06:23.610 --> 00:06:25.789
... and they get to work on sinking the Pearl.

00:06:25.789 --> 00:06:29.909
The windows of the Captain's quarters and
the wood framing them is blown to pieces by

00:06:29.909 --> 00:06:34.930
the Dutchman's cannons, though Sparrow's ship
seems to have some otherworldly qualities

00:06:34.930 --> 00:06:36.029
of its own.

00:06:36.029 --> 00:06:39.500
Either that, or it's a really bad edit.

00:06:39.500 --> 00:06:43.509
Moments later, viewing the Pearl from the
port side of the Dutchman, we see that its

00:06:43.509 --> 00:06:46.449
back end has magically repaired itself.

00:06:46.449 --> 00:06:49.759
"Maybe somebody came in here and fixed it
or something."

00:06:49.759 --> 00:06:52.679
"Someone, like an Angel?"

00:06:52.679 --> 00:06:53.679
Jack's rings

00:06:53.679 --> 00:06:58.139
You know how it was weird that Will Turner
had a Lord of the Rings tattoo?

00:06:58.139 --> 00:07:02.939
Maybe it does make sense — when you consider
that Jack Sparrow has magic rings of his own.

00:07:02.939 --> 00:07:08.490
The Captain is left alone as the mythical
Kraken wraps its giant tentacles around his

00:07:08.490 --> 00:07:12.699
pride and joy, all while he's chained to the
mast of his doomed ship.

00:07:12.699 --> 00:07:18.509
However, to the surprise of absolutely nobody,
Jack manages to escape, using lamp oil to

00:07:18.509 --> 00:07:21.349
wriggle his hand free of his manacle.

00:07:21.349 --> 00:07:26.919
As he does, the rings on his index and ring
fingers come loose and are just about to pop

00:07:26.919 --> 00:07:32.889
right off until Jack whips his hand free,
with the same rings somehow still in place.

00:07:32.889 --> 00:07:34.759
The Fountain of Youth

00:07:34.759 --> 00:07:41.349
As At World's End draws to a close, Jack discovers
that his old nemesis Barbossa has once again

00:07:41.349 --> 00:07:45.259
taken the Black Pearl, though he's able to
continue his search for the Fountain of Youth

00:07:45.259 --> 00:07:46.259
without it.

00:07:46.259 --> 00:07:51.849
As Sparrow sets out from Tortuga on his dinghy,
we see him consulting a scroll that details

00:07:51.849 --> 00:07:58.779
the discovery of 'Aqua de Vida' by someone
named Ponce de León in 1523.

00:07:58.779 --> 00:08:04.659
History buffs know this refers to an actual
Spanish explorer and conquistador who, according

00:08:04.659 --> 00:08:07.310
to legend, set sail in search of the mythical
fountain.

00:08:07.310 --> 00:08:15.059
The trouble is, this voyage actually took
place in the year 1513 — not 1523, as Jack's

00:08:15.059 --> 00:08:16.860
map states.

00:08:16.860 --> 00:08:18.789
So close!

00:08:18.789 --> 00:08:19.909
Tire tracks

00:08:19.909 --> 00:08:25.059
The fourth installment in the franchise, 2011's
On Stranger Tides, begins with Jack making

00:08:25.059 --> 00:08:30.679
a failed attempt to free his first mate Joshamee
Gibbs, who's being held in London on charges

00:08:30.679 --> 00:08:32.039
of piracy.

00:08:32.039 --> 00:08:37.250
Captured, Sparrow is brought before King George
II, who asks him to join his expedition to

00:08:37.250 --> 00:08:39.360
find the Fountain of Youth.

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:43.940
He of course refuses and — as always — makes
an unconventional escape.

00:08:43.940 --> 00:08:47.790
As the hunt for Sparrow moves into the streets
of the British capital, which is filled with

00:08:47.790 --> 00:08:54.050
skinny-wheeled buggies pulled by horses, tire
tracks made by a modern car or truck are visible

00:08:54.050 --> 00:08:56.880
in the mud, as clear as day.

00:08:56.880 --> 00:08:58.760
The shrinking impostor

00:08:58.760 --> 00:09:03.420
Jack soon finds out that an impostor has been
attempting to put together a crew to lead

00:09:03.420 --> 00:09:06.810
an expedition to find the Fountain under his
name.

00:09:06.810 --> 00:09:11.610
When Sparrow comes face to face with said
impostor, the pair become involved in a sword

00:09:11.610 --> 00:09:16.950
fight that keeps the identity of the Captain's
foe hidden until the very last moment.

00:09:16.950 --> 00:09:22.240
The fake Jack turns out to be Anjelica, his
former lover and the daughter of infamous

00:09:22.240 --> 00:09:27.660
pirate Captain Blackbeard, who also happens
to be seeking the mythical Fountain.

00:09:27.660 --> 00:09:31.820
The problem with this scene is the height
difference between Penélope Cruz and whoever

00:09:31.820 --> 00:09:36.800
stood in for her during the battle, as this
person was clearly the same height — if

00:09:36.800 --> 00:09:39.370
not a little taller — than Johnny Depp.

00:09:39.370 --> 00:09:45.590
Meanwhile, Cruz is clearly a few inches shorter
than Depp...even in heels.

00:09:45.590 --> 00:09:46.830
Half-cocked Blackbeard

00:09:46.830 --> 00:09:51.660
Sparrow has various run-ins with Blackbeard
over the course of On Stranger Tides as the

00:09:51.660 --> 00:09:54.540
pair go head to head in a battle of savvy.

00:09:54.540 --> 00:09:59.510
Shanghaied into service aboard his vessel
the Queen Anne's Revenge, Jack fails in an

00:09:59.510 --> 00:10:05.870
attempted mutiny and sees first hand the power
of Blackbeard's legendary Sword of Triton,

00:10:05.870 --> 00:10:10.900
a magic blade that allows the wielder to control
every rope and pulley on the ship.

00:10:10.900 --> 00:10:15.770
"You dare face this sword?"

00:10:15.770 --> 00:10:20.880
But his closest shave with Blackbeard comes
on land, when Jack finds himself with his

00:10:20.880 --> 00:10:23.740
back to a cliff and a gun in his face.

00:10:23.740 --> 00:10:28.800
A closer look reveals that Sparrow isn't actually
in that much danger at all, seeing as Blackbeard

00:10:28.800 --> 00:10:35.500
has his pistol half-cocked and it wouldn't
fire no matter how hard he squeezed the trigger.

00:10:35.500 --> 00:10:37.130
Barbossa's lieutenant commander

00:10:37.130 --> 00:10:42.820
The reveal that Barbossa was alive and well
— minus one leg — perhaps wasn't unexpected,

00:10:42.820 --> 00:10:47.300
nor was the fact that the Black Pearl had
been lost under his command, seeing as he

00:10:47.300 --> 00:10:51.020
seems to lose control of the ship as often
as Jack does.

00:10:51.020 --> 00:10:56.310
The biggest surprise was that Captain Barbossa
was now in the employ of the Crown, though

00:10:56.310 --> 00:11:00.880
either the former pirate isn't up to scratch
with his Navy lingo or the writers made a

00:11:00.880 --> 00:11:03.470
few historical errors.

00:11:03.470 --> 00:11:08.190
Barbossa addresses one of his men as lieutenant
commander, a rank that was not introduced

00:11:08.190 --> 00:11:10.770
into the Royal Navy until 1914.

00:11:10.770 --> 00:11:16.690
Also, his ship flies a version of the Union
Jack flag that didn't exist at the time, as

00:11:16.690 --> 00:11:20.560
the Kingdom of Ireland had yet to be absorbed.

00:11:20.560 --> 00:11:21.960
Magic feathers

00:11:21.960 --> 00:11:26.820
Poor Geoffrey Rush was done yet another disservice
later in the movie, this time by the editing

00:11:26.820 --> 00:11:27.820
department.

00:11:27.820 --> 00:11:33.750
With his ridiculous wig left back on land,
he sheds the rest of his British Naval garb

00:11:33.750 --> 00:11:39.140
after admitting to Jack that his true goal
was to exact revenge on Blackbeard for sinking

00:11:39.140 --> 00:11:41.970
the Pearl and taking his leg in the process.

00:11:41.970 --> 00:11:46.960
The scene in which a young deckhand passes
him a hat more suited to his true standing

00:11:46.960 --> 00:11:51.170
as a pirate is supposed to be a moment for
the character, but instead it leaves viewers

00:11:51.170 --> 00:11:54.070
asking "where the hell did those feathers
come from?"

00:11:54.070 --> 00:11:58.700
Maybe he was just getting ready to set sail
for the player hater's ball after all.

00:11:58.700 --> 00:11:59.780
Thanks for watching!

00:11:59.780 --> 00:12:03.030
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:12:03.030 --> 00:12:05.170
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

